--TEST--
`Core\Process\Result::stdout` and `::stderr` answer a zero-length capture for a child that wrote nothing — the absence edge both members are written around, measured rather than tested for truthiness — `rule:core-classes/process-run`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The other side of the bound `process-a-completed-runs-two-streams-stay-apart.nvst`
// names. That case pins that a stream carries what the child wrote; this one
// pins what a stream carries when the child wrote nothing at all, which is the
// answer neither `stdout` nor `stderr` can arrive at by copying its neighbour.
// `Core\Process::run` builds both members' slots out of
// `Value::bytes(NvsStr::new(&output.stdout))`, so an empty pipe has to survive
// a zero-length allocation, a zero-length `bytes` value and a zero-length read
// back out — three places a capture could pick up a byte it was never given.
//
// The length is **printed**, not asserted to be falsy. The failure this is
// written against is a one-byte answer: a child launched through a shell that
// echoed a line, or a member that terminated the capture itself, hands back
// `"\n"`, which every "is it empty" spelling short of a measurement reads as
// output that is merely small. `0` and `1` are one character apart in the
// expectation and cannot be confused.
//
// A `null` is not the third possibility it looks like: both members are
// declared `bytes` and not `?bytes`, so absence has exactly one spelling here
// and it is the one measured below. That is the decision this case pins in
// place — a later `?bytes` would fail to compile against these declarations
// rather than quietly changing what an empty capture means.
//
// The child writes nothing on purpose and by the shortest route available:
// `:` is the shell's own null command and `exit 0` is `cmd`'s, so neither
// child is a program whose silence depends on how it was invoked. `echo` with
// no argument was the obvious spelling and is precisely wrong — it writes the
// one byte this case exists to refuse.
//
// The two candidates are the platform shells named absolutely, for the reason
// `process-a-completed-run-answers-the-status-the-child-chose.nvst` states:
// § 1 makes a `PATH` lookup the caller's own, and the one that is not this
// platform's starts nothing — `IOError` on Windows, `RuntimeError` for a path
// with no root on Unix, as that case says.

// The sentinels are non-empty, and that is the whole point of them: a run in
// which no child started at all would otherwise print the two zeroes this case
// is looking for and pass without ever having measured a capture.
bytes $out = "sentinel" as bytes;
bytes $err = "sentinel" as bytes;
int $started = 0;

try {
    var $unix = Core\Process::run("/bin/sh", ["-c", ":"]);
    $started = $started + 1;
    $out = $unix->stdout();
    $err = $unix->stderr();
} catch (IOError $notAUnix) {
}

try {
    var $windows = Core\Process::run("C:\\Windows\\System32\\cmd.exe", ["/c", "exit 0"]);
    $started = $started + 1;
    $out = $windows->stdout();
    $err = $windows->stderr();
} catch (RuntimeError $notWindows) {
}

echo "started=", $started, "\n";
echo "stdout length=", Core\Bytes::length($out), "\n";
echo "stderr length=", Core\Bytes::length($err), "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# `true` for the reason the exit-code case gives: the door's own scoping is
# pinned by `process-run-asks-for-the-capability-before-it-looks-at-the-target.nvst`,
# and this case is about what a completed run captured.
[capabilities.process]
exec = true
--EXPECT--
started=1
stdout length=0
stderr length=0
